# Centralised suppressions for Trivy IaC findings on infra/ that we
# have decided not to fix. Each entry lists the rule ID and the
# rationale; remove the entry to re-enable that check.
#
# Format: `AVD-AWS-XXXX` per Trivy's misconfig ID convention. The
# SARIF emits `AWS-XXXX` but the ignore file expects the AVD-prefixed
# form.
#
# Picked up automatically by the Trivy CLI (and the trivy-action) when
# present at the working directory of the scan. The terraform workflow
# checks the repo out at the workspace root, so this file sits there.

# S3 access logging on the Terraform state bucket (infra/bootstrap) and
# the static site bucket (infra/modules/web-stack). State bucket has
# ~1 write/apply audited by CloudTrail data events; site bucket is
# fronted by CloudFront with OAC (direct S3 GETs are blocked) and
# every deploy write lands in CloudTrail too. A second logs bucket
# would duplicate signals already captured upstream.
AVD-AWS-0089

# S3 customer-managed-key encryption on the same two buckets above.
# State bucket has no PII / no customer data — Terraform plans only —
# and is locked to a single OIDC role. Site bucket contains the
# public SvelteKit build, intentionally served anonymously. AES256
# protects against storage-layer compromise; CMK rotation adds no
# marginal security for content with this threat model.
AVD-AWS-0132

# CloudFront access logging. CloudWatch real-time metrics +
# per-Lambda alarms in infra/modules/web-stack/alarms.tf already
# cover the operational signals access logs would. Per-IP audit
# trails aren't a current requirement; revisit if regulatory needs
# change.
AVD-AWS-0010
